{
  "question": "A directed graph has nonnegative edge weights. An A* implementation uses an admissible heuristic that may be inconsistent, reopens a node whenever a strictly smaller path cost is discovered, and terminates when the goal node is removed from the priority queue. Which statement is correct?",
  "options": [
    {
      "A": "A* is guaranteed to return an optimal path because the heuristic is admissible and node reopening is supported.",
      "B": "A* is not guaranteed to return an optimal path unless the heuristic is also consistent.",
      "C": "A* guarantees optimality only when the heuristic is identically zero, in which case it behaves as Dijkstra's algorithm.",
      "D": "A* guarantees optimality only if the heuristic overestimates the remaining cost to the goal."
  ],
  "correct_answer": "A",
  "explanation": "An admissible heuristic never overestimates the true remaining cost. Although inconsistency may cause a node to be expanded more than once, allowing such nodes to be reopened ensures that better paths are reconsidered. Therefore, with nonnegative edge weights and termination when the goal is removed from the priority queue, A* is guaranteed to return an optimal path. Consistency improves efficiency by preventing repeated expansions, but it is not required for optimality when reopening is allowed."
}

{
  "question": "In a five-member Basic Paxos cluster, value X was accepted by exactly two acceptors during ballot 7, so X was never chosen. A new proposer initiates ballot 9 and completes Phase 1 using the other three acceptors, all of which report no previously accepted values. Is it safe for the proposer to select a different value Y for Phase 2?",
  "options": [
    {
      "A": "Yes. X was only accepted, not chosen; because the Phase 1 quorum did not intersect a quorum that accepted X, choosing Y does not violate safety.",
      "B": "No. Paxos must preserve every value that any acceptor has accepted, even if the value was not chosen.",
      "C": "No. A new proposer must always wait until every acceptor has acknowledged the previously accepted value.",
      "D": "Yes, but only because ballot 9 is higher than ballot 7; the Phase 1 responses are irrelevant."
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": "A",
  "explanation": "Paxos safety requires preserving values that may have been chosen, not every value that was merely accepted. Because X was accepted by only two of five acceptors, it was not chosen. The new Phase 1 quorum contains none of those acceptors, so selecting Y is safe. A higher ballot number alone would not make this decision safe; the Phase 1 quorum evidence is essential."
}

